    {
      "id": "bd5d5455-699b-48e4-ad88-46da0f1e2af1",
      "title": "Theology 7 - 5: 9/25 Reflection 2 (2)",
      "description": "You will write this reflection on paper in your class notebook. You will take a picture of it and submit it on MyBelen. This unit has been about how God made the world not just good, but very good, and for our sharing in it. In the beginning there was harmony &#8212; Adam and Eve at peace with God, with themselves, with each other, and with creation. The serpent's voice broke that harmony. That same voice of the serpent, which moves us away from the good and sows doubt in our hearts about who God is, works in your life. But so does God's voice, who never tires of bringing us back.&#160;       Reflection Prompt: Look at your own life.  Step 1 &#8212; Pick one of the four harmonies to write about: harmony with God &#183; harmony with yourself &#183; harmony with others (family, friends) &#183; harmony with creation (the world, your things, how you treat what's around you) Step 2 &#8212; After you pick which harmony to write about, you will go deep into that one. Think through these four questions. Use them to figure out what you want to say:  What does harmony in this relationship look like when it's good? Describe a real moment you've felt it. Where do you feel the serpent's voice trying to break it? What lie does it tell you? (For example: \"you don't need to talk to God,\" \"you're not good enough,\" \"get back at him,\" \"it doesn't matter how you treat this.\") What does it wound or cost you when you listen to that voice? How might God be trying to draw you back into harmony here? What would His voice be asking of you?        Writing Instructions: Your reflection should be at least 150 words. For full credit, your response must do these things:  Answer all four prompt questions.&#160; Write it as one connected paragraph &#8212; not a numbered list. Move through all four questions, but make it read like you're telling me one honest story about this relationship, not answering a quiz.  Use words like but, because, so, even though, lately to connect your thoughts. You can start like this: \"Lately my harmony with my brother has been broken, because the serpent's voice keeps telling me to get back at him whenever he annoys me&#8230;\"   Use at least three of these words in your response: goodness &#183; image and likeness &#183; harmony &#183; temptation &#183; sin &#183; the Fall &#183; wound &#183; freedom. Be honest. Think deeply. This is for you.     How I Grade It &#8212; Four things, 4 points each. 16 points total.     &#160; 4 3 2 1&#8211;0     Your own life Picks one harmony and goes deep &#8212; a real moment I could picture, honest about the lie and what it costs. Real, but stays general &#8212; no moment I could point to. Touches several harmonies shallowly, or stays about people in general instead of you. Doesn't answer the prompt.   The three words Three or more, used correctly, and they fit what you're describing. Three words, but one is used loosely. Only two, or two used correctly. Fewer than two, or used wrong.   Effort &amp; depth Full length. Moves through all four questions, including the hard ones (the wound, God's voice). Reads like one honest reflection, not a list. Full length, but part is filler, or one or two questions are skipped. Reads like a numbered list of disconnected answers, or clearly short of 150 words. A few lines. Written on the way to class.   Grammar</t",
